# tests/assertions/d27/gating.rxt -- MOD-0.3c feature-gating, the refusal
# direction the brief asked for: without --features assertions (or,
# for (?m), the RIGHT combination of modules), each construct must be
# refused naming the missing module; with it, it must compile.
#
# pcrec-specific finding (see ../README.md FINDINGS): (?m) needs BOTH
# 'modifiers' (for the inline-option GROUP SYNTAX itself, already
# default-enabled via std1) and 'assertions' (for the MULTILINE MATCHING
# EFFECT) -- neither module alone is sufficient, and the two failure modes
# report genuinely different diagnostics (measured below). This is a
# two-module construct, unlike the other seven in this corpus, and the
# brief's "each construct... must refuse naming the module" (singular)
# does not quite fit it -- the four gating cells for (?m) below are the
# reason this file documents the shape explicitly rather than folding it
# into a one-line-per-construct table.
#
# Every perr block in this file is `# pcrec-gate-only`: it is pcrec POLICY
# (a module gate), not a PCRE2 syntax fact, so ../oracle.py skips it rather
# than mis-scoring a design choice as if it were a PCRE2 compile verdict
# (libpcre2 has no module system and accepts all eight constructs
# unconditionally at options=0).

# --- \A \z \Z \b \B \G \K: refused without module 'assertions' ---

# \A, DEFAULT features (std1 = classes,modifiers): refused, names module 'assertions'. (bare \A alone, with nothing to match after it, is also refused the same way -- this uses a trailing literal only so the same spelling is reusable as a positive-compile smoke pattern below.)
# pcrec-gate-only
pattern \Ax
perr

# \z, DEFAULT features (std1 = classes,modifiers): refused, names module 'assertions'. (bare \z alone, with nothing to match after it, is also refused the same way -- this uses a trailing literal only so the same spelling is reusable as a positive-compile smoke pattern below.)
# pcrec-gate-only
pattern \zx
perr

# \Z, DEFAULT features (std1 = classes,modifiers): refused, names module 'assertions'. (bare \Z alone, with nothing to match after it, is also refused the same way -- this uses a trailing literal only so the same spelling is reusable as a positive-compile smoke pattern below.)
# pcrec-gate-only
pattern \Zx
perr

# \b, DEFAULT features (std1 = classes,modifiers): refused, names module 'assertions'. (bare \b alone, with nothing to match after it, is also refused the same way -- this uses a trailing literal only so the same spelling is reusable as a positive-compile smoke pattern below.)
# pcrec-gate-only
pattern \bx
perr

# \B, DEFAULT features (std1 = classes,modifiers): refused, names module 'assertions'. (bare \B alone, with nothing to match after it, is also refused the same way -- this uses a trailing literal only so the same spelling is reusable as a positive-compile smoke pattern below.)
# pcrec-gate-only
pattern \Bx
perr

# \G, DEFAULT features (std1 = classes,modifiers): refused, names module 'assertions'. (bare \G alone, with nothing to match after it, is also refused the same way -- this uses a trailing literal only so the same spelling is reusable as a positive-compile smoke pattern below.)
# pcrec-gate-only
pattern \Gx
perr

# \K, DEFAULT features (std1 = classes,modifiers): refused, names module 'assertions'. (bare \K alone, with nothing to match after it, is also refused the same way -- this uses a trailing literal only so the same spelling is reusable as a positive-compile smoke pattern below.)
# pcrec-gate-only
pattern \Kx
perr

# \A, --features none: refused, same as default (assertions was never in std1).
# pcrec-gate-only
pattern \Ax
features none
perr

# \z, --features none: refused, same as default (assertions was never in std1).
# pcrec-gate-only
pattern \zx
features none
perr

# \Z, --features none: refused, same as default (assertions was never in std1).
# pcrec-gate-only
pattern \Zx
features none
perr

# \b, --features none: refused, same as default (assertions was never in std1).
# pcrec-gate-only
pattern \bx
features none
perr

# \B, --features none: refused, same as default (assertions was never in std1).
# pcrec-gate-only
pattern \Bx
features none
perr

# \G, --features none: refused, same as default (assertions was never in std1).
# pcrec-gate-only
pattern \Gx
features none
perr

# \K, --features none: refused, same as default (assertions was never in std1).
# pcrec-gate-only
pattern \Kx
features none
perr

# --- \A \z \Z \b \B \G \K: compile cleanly WITH module 'assertions' ---

# \A compiles and matches with module 'assertions' enabled.
pattern \Ax
features assertions
m "x" 0 1

# \z compiles and matches with module 'assertions' enabled.
pattern x\z
features assertions
m "x" 0 1

# \Z compiles and matches with module 'assertions' enabled.
pattern x\Z
features assertions
m "x" 0 1

# \b compiles and matches with module 'assertions' enabled.
pattern \bx
features assertions
m "x" 0 1

# \B compiles and matches with module 'assertions' enabled.
pattern x\By
features assertions
m "xy" 0 2

# \G compiles and matches with module 'assertions' enabled.
pattern \Gx
features assertions
m "x" 0 1

# \K compiles and matches with module 'assertions' enabled.
pattern x\K
features assertions
m "x" 1 1

# --- (?m): the two-module construct ---

# (?m), DEFAULT features (std1 includes 'modifiers', so the (?m...) GROUP parses) -- but its MULTILINE EFFECT is refused, naming module 'assertions' specifically (not 'modifiers', which is already satisfied). Distinct diagnostic text from the next cell -- see README FINDINGS for both, verbatim.
# pcrec-gate-only
pattern (?m)^a
perr

# (?m), --features none: refused at the GROUP SYNTAX itself this time, naming module 'modifiers' -- a different failure point than the std1-default cell above, because now neither module is enabled.
# pcrec-gate-only
pattern (?m)^a
features none
perr

# (?m), --features modifiers only (no assertions): same failure point as the std1-default cell above (group syntax is fine, multiline effect is refused) -- confirms it is 'modifiers' alone that was already doing the work in the default-features cell, not some other std1 member.
# pcrec-gate-only
pattern (?m)^a
features modifiers
perr

# (?m), --features assertions only (no modifiers): refused at the GROUP SYNTAX -- the mirror of the previous cell, confirming BOTH modules are independently necessary and neither is sufficient alone.
# pcrec-gate-only
pattern (?m)^a
features assertions
perr

# (?m) compiles and matches only with BOTH modules enabled together.
pattern (?m)^a
features modifiers,assertions
m "a" 0 1
